## 3. Prove screen layout (route `/prove/:checkId`, default `check_rack_inlet`)

Header: `check_rack_inlet` · `check_snmp v2.4 (unmodified)` · stage stepper · `Parallel run day 1` · Live/Cached badge.

Sections, top to bottom, each collapsible but open by default:

### 3.1 Rule card (summary; the full card with execution checks lives on the Read screen)
Two columns.
- Left, "What the old check does": `Pages when a rack inlet reading is outside 0–27 °C (warning) or outside 0–32 °C (critical), after 3 consecutive failed checks 1 minute apart. Pages UNKNOWN if the sensor doesn't answer.`
- Right, "What it ignores": `Trends · Neighbouring racks · Whether the sensor agrees with the outlet · Cooling unit power`.
- Badge: `ShieldCheck Verified by execution · 6/6 statements matched the real plugin`.
- Planned failure variant (toggle in Director): `FileWarning Card flagged: card said "critical above 30 °C"; the plugin returned CRITICAL only above 32 °C. Card regenerated.`

### 3.2 Source ↔ rules traceability
Split view. Left: legacy source, read-only (Nagios command definition + service definition + the plugin invocation), monospace, line numbers. Right: the generated Prometheus rules YAML. Hovering or clicking a rule highlights the source lines it replaces (accent bar in the gutter); each rule has a `replaces:` label. Show this YAML (illustrative, but it must look idiomatic):

```yaml
groups:
  - name: check_rack_inlet            # replaces Nagios service "Inlet" on 39 racks
    rules:
      - alert: RackInletWarning
        expr: rack_inlet_celsius > 27 or rack_inlet_celsius < 0   # Nagios range "27" = outside 0..27
        for: 2m                                                   # max_check_attempts=3, retry_interval=1m
        labels: { severity: warning, replaces: "check_rack_inlet:-w 27", kind: must-keep }
      - alert: RackInletCritical
        expr: rack_inlet_celsius > 32 or rack_inlet_celsius < 0
        for: 2m
        labels: { severity: critical, replaces: "check_rack_inlet:-c 32", kind: must-keep }
      - alert: RackInletUnknown
        expr: rack_inlet_read_ok == 0                             # SNMP timeout -> UNKNOWN
        for: 2m
        labels: { severity: warning, replaces: "notification_options u", kind: must-keep }
      - alert: RackInletTrendBreach
        expr: predict_linear(rack_inlet_celsius[5m], 900) > 35
        labels: { severity: warning, found_by: "fault:fan_degraded", kind: improvement }
```

### 3.3 Value coverage strip (the hero visual)
- A full-width SVG with **three stacked bands**, 14 px each, over the value axis −40.0 … 125.0 °C, plus 3 extra cells at the right end: `timeout`, `noSuchObject`, `non-numeric`.
  - Band 1, **Legacy (real plugin)**: the colour of the exit state per value: OK (muted), WARNING (warning), CRITICAL (critical), UNKNOWN (untrusted).
  - Band 2, **New rules (promtool)**: same encoding.
  - Band 3, **Match**: a thin ok tick per matching value, critical for a mismatch. With 0 mismatches it reads as one calm line, which is the point.
- Draw contiguous runs as single rects (not 1,654 elements). Hover shows the exact value: `27.0 °C → legacy OK · new OK ✓` · `27.1 °C → legacy WARNING · new WARNING ✓`.
- Below it, a **zoom lens** on 24–36 °C at 0.1 °C resolution so the 27.0/27.1 and 32.0/32.1 boundaries are visible.
- Summary line in large type: `1,654 of 1,654 inputs match · 0 mismatches · real plugin run 1,654 times in 41 s`.

### 3.4 Timing tests
- `500 generated reading sequences · 0 mismatches · seed 7 · shrinking on`.
- Show 6 small step charts as examples (ramp, step, flap at the threshold, two bad readings then recovery, dropout, slow drift), each tagged `match ✓`.
- Explain the model in one line: `Old side: real plugin decisions + Nagios soft/hard state model (checked against real Nagios Core on 10 traces). New side: promtool test rules.`

### 3.5 One-shot vs Gauntlet (the 8090 moment)
Two columns with identical structure. Left header: `One-shot translation · Nemotron Super, 1 call, no tools`. Right header: `Gauntlet loop · Nemotron Super + verifier, 3 rounds`.

- Left: its own coverage strip, with a **red block over −40.0 … −0.1 °C**, plus summary `3 counterexamples · 400 value mismatches · 1 timing mismatch · 1 error-case mismatch`.
- Right: an all-green strip, `0 counterexamples`.
- Under the left column, three **counterexample cards** (minimal inputs found by shrinking):

| # | Minimal input | Legacy did | One-shot rule did | Why |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | Reading `−0.1 °C` (sensor error value) | CRITICAL | OK | Nagios range `32` means *outside 0..32*; the rule only checked `> 32` |
| 2 | Readings `26, 33, 33, 26` (1 min apart) | No page (soft state, 2 of 3 attempts) | Paged at the first 33 | Behaviour lives in `max_check_attempts=3`, which isn't in the script; the rule had no `for:` |
| 3 | SNMP `timeout` | UNKNOWN, paged on-call | Silent | `notification_options` includes `u`; the rule had no case for missing data |

- A round timeline under the right column: `Round 1: 3 counterexamples → Round 2: 1 (soft state) → Round 3: 0 · 2 min 14 s`. Each round is expandable to show the counterexample fed back to the model.
- The caption under both columns, in large type: **`Same model both times. The difference is the proof.`**

### 3.6 Intentional differences
A list where each item has a justification and evidence link:
1. `Adds RackInletTrendBreach`: fires earlier on rising trends; never removes a legacy alert. Evidence: `fault: fan_degraded, 9 min earlier`.
2. `Routes alerts from B7 to "Sensor fault" until the sensor is replaced`: the alert is still raised (parity holds), but it goes to the right queue. Evidence: accepted finding #1.
3. `Pages go to the on-call roster instead of rkowalski@`. Evidence: accepted finding #5.

Every intentional difference must point at an **accepted** finding from the Find screen. A dismissed or open finding never changes the rules.

